    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d610">

                <pb facs="0635" n="623" sameAs="frus:frus1945v04/pg623"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00116 E. W./12–2245: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Finland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Hulley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Helsinki</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-12-22">December 22,
                        1945</date>.<lb/>[Received December 23—10:11 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>795. Press 22d. After lengthy deliberation, war trial court<anchor n="70"
                        xml:id="d610-f1"/> ordered seven accused imprisoned; Kukkonen alone
                    remaining at <pb facs="0636" n="624" xml:id="pg624"/> liberty. Eight justices
                    voted for imprisoning Reinikka and Kivimaki as compared with four [on] previous
                    occasions. Ten voted for rearresting Tanner as compared with six previously.
                    Four voted for rearresting Kukkonen both times. Eleven voted for keeping Ryti,
                    Rangell, Ramsay and Linkomies in custody as compared with eight previously.</p>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Helsingen Sanomat</hi><anchor n="71" xml:id="d610-f2"/>
                    comments “Court decision yesterday calculated to soft-pedal Soviet criticism
                    sent from here in reports on trial and ease tension that surrounded question
                    politically since end first phase of trial. Easing of tension will be regarded
                    as relief by responsible Finnish authorities.”</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . .</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hulley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d610-f1" n="70" xml:id="d610-f1-content">
                <p>The special war trial court was composed of 15 justices. Three of these were
                    judges from other Finnish courts, and the remaining 12 were members of the
                    Finnish Diet who were chosen by the Diet on the basis of proportional
                    representation.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d610-f2" n="71" xml:id="d610-f2-content">
                <p>A Helsinki daily newspaper affiliated with the National Progressive Party.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
